<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="vitamin E biosynthesis (tocotrienols)" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Description>The term |FRAME: Vitamin-E vitamin E| (tocochromenols) is a collective term for the eight forms of |FRAME: Tocotrienols tocotrienols| and |FRAME: Tocopherols tocopherols|, each existing as &amp;alpha;, &amp;beta;, &amp;gamma;, and &amp;delta; species. Structurally tocotrienols and tocopherols differ only in the degree of unsaturation of their side-chain. The tocotrienol chain comprises three &lt;i&gt;trans&lt;/i&gt;-double bonds, whereas the tocopherol chain is fully saturated. In addition, the &amp;alpha;, &amp;beta;, &amp;gamma;, and &amp;delta; species of both compound groups vary in the methylation pattern of the chromanol ring |CITS:[12897790]|. The tocotrienols are mostly found in monocots |CITS:[Padley94]| and a few dicots |CITS:[17110122]| whereas tocopherols are wider spread in the plant kingdom and dominate in the dicots |CITS:[8827691]|. Tocotrienols are known as potent antioxidant and because of that have been appreciated for their nutritive value in human food and animal fodder |CITS:[8464850]|. Moreover, health-promoting assets of tocotrienols such as anticancer properties |CITS:[22095072]| made this compound group commercially interesting as nutraceuticals. The biosynthetic pathway of tocotrienols (this pathway) only differs in one step from the biosynthesis of tocopherols (see |FRAME:PWY-1422 vitamin E biosynthesis (tocopherols)|). The first committed step of the pathway catalyzed by the homogentisate geranylgeranyl transferase (HGGT) is characterized by the high acceptance of |FRAME:GERANYLGERANYL-PP geranylgeranyl diphosphate| over the similar |FRAME:PHYTYL-PYROPHOSPHATE phytyl diphosphate| which leads the way into tocopherol biosynthesis |CITS:[12897790] [21223386]|. All other enzymes after this step, i.e. tocopherol cyclases |CITS:[18810749] [23733887]| and tocopherol methyltransferases |CITS:[22763462]| can work with either one of the precursors and produce either tocotrienols, tocopherols or a mixture of both. This could also be demonstrated by introducing barley HGGT to species which do not produce tocotrienols, e.g. &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis&lt;/i&gt;, corn and tobacco. As a result of the transformation the transgenic plants produced predominantly tocotrienols and enhanced the overall production of vitamin E in those species |CITS:[12897790] [21223386] [18810749] [526275]|.</Description>
